note Translated from cpython Lib/bisect.py, bisect_right.
note Return the index where to insert item x in list a, assuming a is sorted.
note So if x already appears in the list, inserting at the result puts it just after the rightmost x already there.

to bisect_right x in a from lo below hi with key
    note GAP: type parameter bounds - example 9 lets T appear in `given value is a T`, but nothing says T and K must support `is less than` (Python relies on __lt__), so the two lines below are invented
    given T is an Ordered type
    given K is an Ordered type
    given a is a List of T
    note GAP: x is a T when key is absent and a K when key is present; the doc cannot make a parameter's type depend on whether another argument is given
    given x is a K
    note GAP: default and keyword-only arguments - Python's lo=0, hi=None and *, key=None have no Scribe form, so every caller passes all three, writing 0, nothing and nothing for the defaults
    given lo is an Int
    given hi is a maybe Int
    note GAP: operations as values - the doc has no type for an operation passed as an argument, so `Operation from T to K` is invented
    given key is a maybe Operation from T to K
    produces an Int
    fails with ValueError
    expect a is sorted because "assuming a is sorted"
    note UNCLEAR: `is sorted` is defined in example 11 only for a List of Int, and cannot say "sorted by key"
    note UNCLEAR: the docstring's guarantee is stated for the whole list, so it holds only for lo 0 and hi nothing; the `if ..., for every ..., ...` comma nesting is not settled by the doc
    ensure if key is nothing and lo is 0 and hi is nothing, for every j from 0 until result, item j of a is at most x
    ensure if key is nothing and lo is 0 and hi is nothing, for every j from result until count of a, item j of a is more than x

    if lo is less than 0
        note UNCLEAR: `because` is defined for clauses; the doc does not say whether a `fail with` line may carry a message
        fail with ValueError because "lo must be non-negative"
    note WORKAROUND: Python rebinds the parameters lo and hi; Scribe parameters are read-only, so the moving bounds get new names
    var low starts at lo
    note WORKAROUND: there is no "value or default" expression for a maybe; choose is a statement, so high is set in both branches
    var high starts at count of a
    choose hi
        when some given_hi
            set high to given_hi
        when nothing
            set high to count of a
    note Note, the comparison uses "<" to match the __lt__() logic in list.sort() and in heapq.
    note UNCLEAR: the doc uses `/` on Int in example 10 as if it rounds down, but never says so; Python's // floors
    note UNCLEAR: Python ints cannot overflow; with Scribe's checked arithmetic low + high could fail, and no failure for it is declared
    note UNCLEAR: if hi is more than count of a, Python raises IndexError at a[mid]; the doc gives no failure for `item` out of range
    choose key
        when nothing
            repeat while low is less than high
                decreases high - low
                let mid be (low + high) / 2
                if x is less than item mid of a
                    set high to mid
                otherwise
                    set low to mid + 1
        when some key_function
            repeat while low is less than high
                decreases high - low
                let mid be (low + high) / 2
                note GAP: calling an operation held in a variable - written like an ordinary call, but the doc never shows a bound name being called
                if x is less than key_function (item mid of a)
                    set high to mid
                otherwise
                    set low to mid + 1
    give back low
